{{nihongo|'''Hinomae'''|火野前|Hinomae}} is a village located within the town of [[Minamiise, Kagami|Minamiise]] in [[Kagami Prefecture]], on the island of [[Yagikoshu]] in [[Okaiken]]. Situated at the southwesternmost tip of the [[Suwa Peninsula]], it is the westernmost settlement in Okaiken. The village is sandwiched between [[Mount Ryūshutsu]], a dormant steep-sloped composite volcano, and the southwestern face of the [[Yagikoshu Alps]]. Hinomae is internationally renowned as the sole viable cultivation site of {{nihongo|'''''Oryza incaensus'''''|巨米|Ōkome}}, the giant rice tree commonly called the Hinomae rice tree—a species that grows up to fifty times larger than its closest relative ''[[Oryza sativa]]''. The species is endangered in the wild and protected under Okaiken's [[Species Act of 2015]].

The name of the village comes from the [[Sanese language|Sanese]] phrase {{nihongo|''Hi no mae''|火の前|Hi no mae}} meaning "before the fire," referring to its geographic location at the foot of a volcano. The area near the volcano remained uninhabited until the end of the medieval period, when the samurai [[Michinaga Yorimichi]] claimed the territory as part of the ''[[kokudaka]]'' land surveying system. A small settlement of [[Jōdō-shū]] monks and followers later occupied the site, establishing the terraced rice paddies and irrigation channels that still function today. As of April 2025, the village has a population of 487.

Hinomae is known across [[Adonia]] for its rice products. The grains of ''Oryza incaensus''—marketed commercially as {{nihongo|'''Hinomae Ōkome'''|火野前巨米|Hinomae Ōkome}}—are among the most expensive and sought-after grain products in the world, commanding prices up to ten times that of standard premium rice. The village is also known for the {{nihongo|'''Ōkome Matsuri'''|巨米祭|Giant Rice Festival}}, a ceremony held every twelve years in which a single rice tree is cut and carried in procession up Mount Ryūshutsu as an offering to the [[Amida Buddha]].

==Geography==
[[File:Hinomae Rice Terraces.png|thumb|left|250px|The terraced rice paddies of Hinomae, carved into the mountainside over centuries, with the Ōkome grove visible on the ridge above.]]


Hinomae is located at the southwesternmost tip of the [[Suwa Peninsula]] on the island of [[Yagikoshu]]. The village sits on the floor of a steep valley that separates [[Mount Ryūshutsu]] from the southwest face of [[Mount Hino]], part of the [[Yagikoshu Alps]] range. The settlement is bordered on its western edge by the [[Sea of Mitoyagi]], placing it at the maritime frontier between Okaiken and the People's Republic of [[Shansi]].

The area's geological history is defined by volcanic activity. The valley floor was submerged beneath the sea until approximately 45,000 BCE, when a massive eruption of Mount Ryūshutsu filled the eastern portion separating the volcano from the Yagikoshu mainland, creating the land that is now occupied by Hinomae. Today, the village sits an average of four metres above sea level, with the [[Hinomae River]]—the primary water source for the rice trees—running through the village before emptying at the northern shore.

The landscape is riddled with giant volcanic boulders ejected by Mount Ryūshutsu during its last eruption more than 2,000 years ago. Although the valley slopes are steep, the valley bottom has remained remarkably flat, making it ideally suited for rice cultivation. The rich, fertile black volcanic soils—enriched by millennia of ash deposits—are the key factor enabling the extraordinary growth of ''Oryza incaensus'', which reaches heights of 4–6 metres, roughly fifty times the biomass of standard rice.

Due to its remote location, Hinomae is accessible only by [[Okaiken National Route 1|Route 1]] of the Okaiken Highways system. The closest airport is in [[Sekiguchi]], approximately 98 miles from the village centre.

==Ōkome (Giant Rice Tree)==
{{Main|Oryza incaensus}}
{{nihongo|'''Ōkome'''|巨米|lit. "giant rice"}}, scientifically classified as '''''Oryza incaensus''''', is a woody, tree-like rice cultivar endemic to the volcanic valleys of southwestern Yagikoshu. Unlike standard rice (''Oryza sativa''), which grows as a herbaceous grass, ''O. incaensus'' develops a rigid, lignified stalk reaching 4–6 metres in height, with grain heads up to 80 cm in length yielding over 2,000 grains per stalk. The plant's extraordinary size is attributed to the unique mineral composition of the volcanic black soils—particularly the high concentrations of phosphorus and iron from Mount Ryūshutsu's ash deposits—and to centuries of selective breeding by the Jōdō-shū monastic community that originally settled the valley.

The cultivar is now critically endangered in the wild and survives almost exclusively in the terraced paddies of Hinomae and the immediately surrounding valley floor. The [[Species Act of 2015]] formally designated ''O. incaensus'' as a protected species, making it illegal to export live specimens, seeds, or genetic material without authorization from the [[Okaiken Ministry of Agriculture]]. Despite this protection, poaching remains a persistent concern; in 2023, three Shansian nationals were arrested attempting to smuggle 12 kilograms of Ōkome seed out of the country via the [[Sekiguchi]] ferry terminal.

Hinomae Ōkome is reserved almost exclusively for ceremonial and diplomatic use. The [[Imperial House of Okaiken]] receives the first harvest each year as a formal tribute, and the grain is served at state banquets, imperial weddings, and the annual [[Cult of Iron|Iron Ascent]] ceremony on [[Mount Asuka]]. A limited commercial release of approximately 200 kilograms per year is sold through licensed dealers, with individual 5-kilogram bags retailing for over 円150,000.

===Ōkome Matsuri===
[[File:Hinomae Okome Matsuri.png|thumb|right|300px|The Ōkome Matsuri harvest festival, with villagers carrying ceremonial rice bundles through the terraced paddies.]]

=
The {{nihongo|'''Ōkome Matsuri'''|巨米祭|Giant Rice Festival}} is a religious and cultural festival held every twelve years in Hinomae, most recently in 2024. The centerpiece of the festival is the ceremonial cutting of a single mature rice tree—selected by the abbot of the Hinomae Jōdō-shū temple—which is then carried in a procession of over 1,000 pilgrims up the slopes of Mount Ryūshutsu to a shrine at the 1,200-metre elevation, where it is offered to the Amida Buddha as a prayer for volcanic restraint and agricultural abundance. The festival draws over 200,000 visitors to the remote valley, temporarily multiplying the village's population by a factor of 400 and requiring the deployment of [[Kagami Prefectural Police]] and [[Sanese Imperial Forces]] personnel for crowd control and emergency medical services.

The 2024 Ōkome Matsuri gained additional national attention when [[Crown Prince Tomohito]] attended the ceremony, becoming the first member of the Imperial House to visit Hinomae since the [[Jutsu Restoration]]. His presence was widely interpreted as a signal of the imperial family's commitment to rural preservation and the protection of Okaiken's agricultural heritage.

==Economy==
The economy of Hinomae is almost entirely dependent on the cultivation and processing of ''Oryza incaensus''. The village operates under a cooperative model managed by the {{nihongo|Hinomae Ōkome Agricultural Cooperative|火野前巨米農業協同組合|Hinomae Ōkome Nōgyō Kyōdō Kumiai}}, which coordinates planting, harvesting, and distribution among the village's 34 registered farming households. The cooperative also manages the limited commercial retail operation and negotiates the annual imperial tribute.

Secondary economic activities include small-scale tourism, particularly during the Ōkome Matsuri cycle, and the production of {{nihongo|'''kasa-zao'''|傘竿|kasa-zao}}—the heavy-duty rain capes woven from dried Ōkome stalks that are worn by farmers throughout the Yagikoshu monsoon season. These capes have become a niche fashion item in urban markets, with designer versions sold in [[Okami]] boutiques for over 円50,000.

==Transportation==
Hinomae's extreme remoteness is both a defining characteristic and a persistent economic challenge. The village is accessible only via [[Okaiken National Route 1]], a winding mountain road that traverses the Suwa Peninsula. The nearest rail connection is at [[Minamiise Station]], approximately 45 kilometres away. The nearest airport is [[Sekiguchi Airport]], 98 miles from the village centre. During the monsoon season, Route 1 is frequently closed by landslides, cutting the village off from overland supply for periods of up to two weeks.

Proposals to extend the [[Shinkansen]] network or construct a regional airport closer to the Suwa Peninsula have been repeatedly rejected by the Kagami Prefectural Assembly on environmental grounds, as the peninsula contains several protected volcanic habitats and nesting grounds for the endangered [[Sanese crested ibis]].

==In popular culture==
Hinomae and the Ōkome have appeared as settings and motifs in several Sanese cultural works. The village served as the primary location for the 2019 Taisen drama {{nihongo|'''''The Last Harvest'''''|最後の収穫|Saigo no Shūkaku}}, which dramatized a fictional landslide that threatens the final Ōkome crop before the Matsuri. The drama was a ratings success, drawing 28 million viewers for its finale and sparking a national conversation about rural depopulation and agricultural preservation. The {{nihongo|Kurayami-e|暗絵|Shadow Picture}} animated film {{nihongo|'''''Roots of Fire'''''|火の根|Hi no Ne}} (2022) used the Ōkome as a metaphor for Sanese resilience, depicting the rice trees as sentient guardians of the volcanic valley.

